# End-to-end EMULATOR test for the BIGTREETECH Octopus Pro v1.0
# (STM32H723) in DETERMINISTIC TICK MODE. Boots klipper firmware under
# Renode against the Octopus Pro (H723) pin map, completes klippy
# identify + clocksync, brings up the full MCU config (4 TMC5160s on a
# software SPI bus, software-PWM heaters, fan, endstops, 2 thermistors),
# reads the thermistors through ADC1, round-trips the four TMC5160
# register files over SPI, and exercises interactive gcode (M114/M105)
# against real firmware.
#
# This is the STM32H7 per-printer test. It is the SPI sibling of
# duet3_6hc (SAME70 TMC5160) - same renode_hooks TMC SPI chain responder
# (CPU-PC hook on spidev_transfer: read MOSI from the firmware buffer,
# synthesise the MISO datagram with the one-transaction-delay model, then
# PC-skip the real transfer) - but driven over klipper SOFTWARE SPI
# rather than hardware USART-SPI. klipper's src/stm32/spi.c has no
# hardware-SPI bus for MACH_STM32H7, so H7 boards bit-bang SPI in
# firmware; both paths funnel through spidev_transfer (src/spicmds.c),
# which the responder hooks, so it serves the software path unchanged.
# DUMP_TMC reads back the live register file for stepper_x and extruder,
# IOIN version=0x30 proving the SPI read path round-tripped.
#
# The other new ground is the H7 ADC: thermistors on ADC1 channel 10
# (PC0, extruder) and channel 13 (PC3, bed), read end to end by M105 via
# test/emulator/stm32h7_adc_stub.py - a Python.PythonPeripheral that
# completes klipper's H7 ISR/CR/SQR1/DR ADC handshake
# (src/stm32/stm32h7_adc.c). Upstream Renode models the H7 ADC at
# 0x40022000 with the F0-family Analog model, which neither matches that
# handshake nor returns controllable values (and would spin the firmware
# calibration loop), so the vendored stm32h723.repl omits it and the
# launcher maps this stub there instead (0x400 window also covers the
# ADC12 common CCR at +0x308).
#
# As with the other per-printer tests, the gated path is MOTION-FREE:
# tick mode resolves the renode wall-clock-vs-virtual-time drift (a full
# SPI + ADC config free-running on the 520 MHz H7 drifts the clocksync
# and drops the connection mid-init), but motion commands (homing) are
# delivered by klippy's wall-clock-paced serialqueue background thread,
# which under shared-host CPU contention can deliver a tightly-scheduled
# stepper command late and trip "Timer too close". The boot / config /
# TMC-init / ADC / DUMP_TMC / M114 / M105 path schedules no such
# tight-lead commands and is deterministically green. The firmware is the
# generic serial-mode stm32h723 build (stm32h723.dict); the real board is
# USB+CAN, which the STM32H7 Renode platform does not model.
DICTIONARY stm32h723.dict
CONFIG octopus_pro_h723.cfg
EMULATOR octopus_pro_h723.fixture.json
REQUIRES_EMULATOR  # deterministic-time lockstep needs the real backend

M114
M105
DUMP_TMC STEPPER=stepper_x
DUMP_TMC STEPPER=extruder

EXPECT_LOG_CONTAINS "Loaded MCU 'mcu'"
EXPECT_LOG_CONTAINS "Configured MCU 'mcu'"
# DUMP_TMC dumped two drivers; IOIN version=0x30 proves the software-SPI
# read path round-tripped through the responder.
EXPECT_LOG_CONTAINS "DUMP_TMC stepper_x"
EXPECT_LOG_CONTAINS "DUMP_TMC extruder"
EXPECT_LOG_CONTAINS "version=0x30"
EXPECT_LOG_NOT_CONTAINS "Unable to write tmc spi"
EXPECT_LOG_NOT_CONTAINS "Unable to read tmc spi"
EXPECT_LOG_NOT_CONTAINS "failed to init"
EXPECT_LOG_NOT_CONTAINS "Internal error"
EXPECT_LOG_NOT_CONTAINS "Got EOF when reading from device"
# A broken ADC stub surfaces as a flat-zero / out-of-range thermistor.
EXPECT_LOG_NOT_CONTAINS "ADC out of range"
EXPECT_LOG_NOT_CONTAINS "Timer too close"
EXPECT_LOG_NOT_CONTAINS "Rescheduled timer in the past"
EXPECT_LOG_NOT_CONTAINS "Klippy is shutdown"
